1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,078
You know how n8n works, what a system prompt is, what draft-only means at the node

2
00:00:05,078 --> 00:00:05,423
level.

3
00:00:05,423 --> 00:00:08,075
None of that belongs in front of your manager.

4
00:00:08,075 --> 00:00:12,630
They need three things: what it does, what it's worth, and what could go wrong.

5
00:00:12,630 --> 00:00:17,473
Everything else is for you, not for them, however satisfying it would be to explain.

6
00:00:17,473 --> 00:00:20,413
Why does "how" actively hurt, rather than just pad?

7
00:00:20,413 --> 00:00:24,968
Because a decision-maker cannot verify it, and unverifiable detail creates work

8
00:00:24,968 --> 00:00:26,236
instead of confidence.

9
00:00:26,236 --> 00:00:30,848
Every node name on the page is a thing Marcus cannot assess, which quietly turns

10
00:00:30,848 --> 00:00:35,518
into a question he has to either ask or swallow, and a page that makes its reader

11
00:00:35,518 --> 00:00:38,113
feel unqualified gets deferred, not approved.

12
00:00:38,113 --> 00:00:42,725
Deferral is how small good ideas die: not rejected, just moved to a meeting that

13
00:00:42,725 --> 00:00:43,878
never quite happens.

14
00:00:43,878 --> 00:00:45,492
The cut is not dumbing down.

15
00:00:45,492 --> 00:00:50,220
It is moving the conversation onto the only ground where the reader is the expert:

16
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:51,949
whether the trade is worth it.

17
00:00:52,499 --> 00:00:57,596
This is a genuinely hard cut for anyone who just spent ten sections learning how the

18
00:00:57,596 --> 00:00:59,416
machinery works, you included.

19
00:00:59,416 --> 00:01:04,330
The instinct to explain it is exactly the instinct a one-pager has to resist, and

20
00:01:04,330 --> 00:01:09,305
the consolation is real: everything you cut still exists, in your notes, ready for

21
00:01:09,305 --> 00:01:12,702
the one meeting in ten where somebody actually asks how.

22
00:01:13,252 --> 00:01:14,625
That is the whole test.

23
00:01:14,625 --> 00:01:19,282
Does this sentence answer what it does, what it saved, or what could go wrong?

24
00:01:19,282 --> 00:01:24,118
If it does not do one of those three things, it belongs in your own notes, not on

25
00:01:24,118 --> 00:01:28,356
the page you actually hand over to someone deciding whether to say yes.

26
00:01:28,356 --> 00:01:31,401
Run two real sentences through it to feel the edge.

27
00:01:31,401 --> 00:01:36,416
"The agent uses a Switch node to route on the model's verdict": answers how, cut it,

28
00:01:36,416 --> 00:01:38,088
however proud of it you are.

29
00:01:38,088 --> 00:01:42,745
"If it misjudges an email, the worst case is a flag I disagree with at review,

30
00:01:42,745 --> 00:01:47,461
nothing reaches a client": answers what could go wrong, and stays, jargon-free,

31
00:01:47,461 --> 00:01:48,595
exactly as written.

32
00:01:48,595 --> 00:01:53,610
Notice the second sentence is actually the more technical achievement, wearing plain

33
00:01:53,610 --> 00:01:54,088
clothes.

34
00:01:54,088 --> 00:01:56,595
That is the register the whole page wants.

35
00:01:57,145 --> 00:02:00,586
The two checks in that list earn their place differently.

36
00:02:00,586 --> 00:02:05,596
The ninety second read-aloud is a length gate that cannot be argued with, because a

37
00:02:05,596 --> 00:02:10,304
busy reader gives you about that long before deciding whether to keep reading.

38
00:02:10,304 --> 00:02:15,194
The naive-reader test catches the subtler failure: words you no longer notice are

39
00:02:15,194 --> 00:02:19,057
jargon, because ten sections of this course made them furniture.

40
00:02:19,057 --> 00:02:24,067
Whatever your test reader stumbles on, the stumble is the edit list, delivered free

41
00:02:24,067 --> 00:02:27,447
by the one reviewer who cannot be fooled by familiarity.

42
00:02:27,447 --> 00:02:32,457
Next lecture is where you actually fill the page in, using C10 and your own numbers

43
00:02:32,457 --> 00:02:33,001
from C09.
